why don't we start though with your big  speech today and the big announcement  contained therein half a billion pounds  to get people into work  yes good morning neil i said uh over a  year and a half ago at the beginning of  this crisis that my economic priority  was going to be people's jobs and  today's announcement doubles down on  that a year ago we outlined our plan for  jobs and since then the labor market has  performed incredibly strongly if you  just think back people were expecting  the unemployment rate to reach 12  and thankfully it's less than five  percent it's been falling for months now  more and more people getting into work  record numbers of job vacancies you know  that shows me that our plan for jobs is  working which is why today we're  extending that plan for jobs into next  year to keep supporting people all of  which does suggest though chancellor and  people will be will be very pleased that  there is more money to assist people  getting back into work particularly  those who are perhaps slightly older and  looking for work but doesn't all of this  suggest that with the end of furlough we  will have to expect some pretty  significant rises in unemployment  i said right at the beginning of this  crisis it wasn't going to be possible  for me or quite frankly any chancellor  to save every single person's job but  what i do know is that the interventions  that we put in place have made an  enormous difference as i said at the  beginning of this crisis people thought  the unemployment rate would get to 12  that's millions and millions of people  out of work in fact the unemployment  rate has now been falling for about six  months in a row it's under five percent  we have a lower unemployment rate here  in the uk than america canada france  spain italy amongst others and there are  record numbers of job vacancies so i  think the plan is working now of course  some people are sadly have lost their  job and will lose their job but that's  why i want them to be reassured that we  are throwing literally the kitchen sink  at helping them get a new job new skills  new opportunities and we know that  that's been working over the past 12 to  18 months and that's why we're doing  more of it today  uh clearly as mentioned in the  introduction you are speaking to us from  manchester from the conservative party  conference and one thing i am sure  practically every attendee would wish to  hear from the chancellor and they did  the prime minister is that there won't  be any more tax rises the manifesto  promise has been broken once can you  guarantee that it won't be broken again  well it was a very difficult decision to  put up people's taxes no chancellor  wants to do that certainly no  conservative chancellor difficult for me  difficult for the prime minister but  just remember why we did that that was  to make sure that our nhs could get the  significant resources that it needs to  help recover strongly from coronavirus  i've missed operations i'll just get on  to that and also alongside that to usher  in some reforms to social care that  governments have ducked for decades and  this prime minister deserves enormous  credit for gripping those and  implementing uh some changes there not  least to invest in our social care  workforce to make sure that they get the  recognition that they deserve and those  things are significant investments 12  billion pounds and we need to pay for  those and that's why we took the  difficult but i believe responsible  decision to do what we did  sure but but at the same time we are  hearing from the local government  association that they are going to have  to see council tax rises perhaps as  large coming close to double figures  nine percent to plug the black hole in  in social care i mean can you guarantee  that we won't have to see a rise in  council taxes  well social care is funded by a mix of  different ways one way is through local  taxes like council tax and the adult  social care precept now that's existed  for years that's one part of the funding  system and the other part of can you  guarantee that  and we know this well i wouldn't i  wouldn't i can't i can't yes and later  in the year we have something called the  local government finance settlement neil  where the secretary of state for local  government sets out all the plans for  local government for the coming year it  wouldn't be right for me to preempt that  but but what people should know is we  want to put more money into social care  that's why we took the decisions that we  did there will be as i said these are  landmark reforms to cap the catastrophic  costs that people will be liable for  more investment in the social care  workforce more integration between the  nhs and social care and as i said at the  beginning significant investment in the  nhs to make sure that we recover  strongly and work our way through the  backlogs let's talk supply chain then  just  for a brief second  it has been reported that figures from  the industry met with representatives  from the government back in june  about the risks the issues of the issues  within the supply chain they warned  about potential numbers did that meeting  not take place  or did the government just ignore that  they had been warned that this was all  coming down the pipeline  so i'm not obviously aware of the  particular circumstances of the meeting  that you're describing but what i would  say just to put this in context now of  course we're grappling with some  challenges in supply chains uh that you  that you highlighted as did your report  i think the first thing to say is it's  not just here these things are happening  all around the world because of the way  that economies everywhere are recovering  reasonably rapidly from coronavirus  after a period of of having been closed  down that's causing some adjustment  issues which all of us around the world  are working through and with regard to  hgv drivers you'll 