here isn't  there is an upside to these big  supermarket supply chains being broken  up i mean if that's the case what is it  so i didn't quite understand your  question  upside into what sorry neil the upside  into the breakup of the supermarket  supply chains  a breakup of the supply chains  well i don't think anyone would say that  there's an upside to  you know people struggling with supply  chain challenges and not being able to  get what they want at the time they  wanted no one wants to see that i think  what what we have said more broadly is  over time you know one of our desires is  to transition ourselves to a higher wage  high skill more productive economy i  think that is a good thing that's part  of what today's plan for jobs  announcement is about in fact it's  giving people the skills they need to  get well paid jobs now over time that's  that's the type of economy we want to  have which is why people don't want to  just pull as a prime minister we don't  want just to pull the lever on more  unskilled migration now in the short  term that's a pragmatic response to some  of the things we're seeing but longer  term we do want to have people in  well-paid jobs and i think i think  that's probably the point uh whether it  was my colleagues or others have been  trying to make indeed i'm just just on  friday in an interview the prime  minister said this  never mind life expectancy never mind  cancer outcomes  look at wage growth what what does it  say of this prime minister's priorities  that he issues you know concerns about  rises in cancer diagnosis he issues  concerns about you know life expectancy  dropping in favor of wages  no i think that was a  mischaracterization of of what he said  and you started this interview earlier  asking me about tax and and tax rises  you know this is a prime minister and a  government that has taken that difficult  decision on tax precisely because we  want to improve cancer diagnosis and  catch up on all the missed appointments  that people had and just two days ago we  announced i think something like 40 new  community diagnostic hubs across the  united kingdom to do exactly that and  early screening and diagnosis of things  like cancer through mri through  ultrasound because we are committed to  people's health i think we've seen that  over the last year and year and a half  and as this interview started with you  know we're prepared to do some quite  difficult things on the tax side to make  sure the nhs is well-funded and and to  do so that we can do all the things that  you just talked about  just just on taxation though once again  we've seen a fairly significant data  dump of papers this time referred to as  the as the pandora papers pointing out  the president's prime ministers at  billionaires are all taking advantage of  offshore uh tax regimes  leaving that to one side i just want to  ask a very straightforward question have  you ever benefited from an offshore  arrangement  no no i i haven't and i think i've seen  these things overnight as well and it's  always tough for me to comment on them  specifically given uh they've only just  emerged and of course hmrc will look  through those to see if there's  something  we can learn but broadly people should  know that we have a very good track  record on on exactly this type of  activity and tackling it we've led the  world in improving the sharing of  information between tax authorities law  enforcement agencies registers of what  are called beneficial ownership it's  something that we you know we are proud  leaders of here in the uk but as as the  paper showed this is a global phenomenon  there's a global dimension to it we can  do our bit but we require other  countries to cooperate with us as well  and you will  continue to expect us to press on that  indeed and i wonder if i can just again  end on on a slightly personal note  you'll have noticed the advice coming  from the police in london um about how  women should behave telling them that  they should flag down a bus if stopped  by a suspicious police officer i mean  you've got daughters yourself they're  quite young but in a few years time they  will be out of the family home i just  wonder what you make  of that advice being given to your  daughter  well  of course we all should be able to trust  the police but the fact that you're even  asking this question i think  demonstrates to me and to everyone that  there's a problem and we need to address  it and you know you've heard lots of  people talk about some of the specific  things we're doing but it probably  starts with everyone and especially men  acknowledging what a problem this is and  making sure that we recognize that  anxiety as you said i have two young  daughters you know wayne cousins was  roaming around an area where my home in  london outside of downing street is you  know brought this home  we have to recognize what a challenge  this is and and work very hard to  address people's fears

